1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,400
Least squares is a lamppost: the geometry is explicit, so we can watch exactly what optimization does.

2
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:19,866
The loss is the squared length of the residual, and its gradient is two X transpose times the residual.

3
00:00:19,866 --> 00:00:25,333
A gradient step with learning rate eta subtracts eta times that gradient.

4
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:32,266
Expand it and the step is a fixed linear map applied to the weights, plus a constant.

5
00:00:32,266 --> 00:00:39,400
Subtract the minimizer and the constant vanishes: the error is multiplied by the same matrix at every step.

6
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:47,200
Optimizing a quadratic is therefore a linear dynamical system, and its eigenvalues decide everything.

7
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:55,800
In the eigenbasis of X transpose X, a problem splits into scalar ones. Start with one, sigma times w equals y.

8
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:02,833
Each gradient step multiplies the error by the same number, r equals one minus two eta sigma squared.

9
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:09,166
A small learning rate gives r between zero and one: the error shrinks smoothly, but slowly.

10
00:01:09,166 --> 00:01:16,933
At eta equal to one over two sigma squared, r is zero, and a single step lands exactly on the answer.

11
00:01:16,933 --> 00:01:25,200
Push eta higher and r turns negative: the iterate overshoots, bounces across the answer, and still settles.

12
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:33,300
Past eta equal to one over sigma squared, r falls below minus one, and every bounce is bigger than the last.

13
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:41,700
So the scalar rule is simple: the iteration is stable exactly when the absolute value of r is below one.

14
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:48,500
Now two directions: a ravine, steep where sigma is 2 and shallow where sigma is one half.

15
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:57,900
Take eta equal to 0.2. Each step multiplies the steep coordinate by minus 0.6 and the shallow one by 0.9.

16
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:03,866
The path bounces across the ravine while creeping along it: the shallow direction sets the pace.

17
00:02:03,866 --> 00:02:11,566
The steepest direction forbids a bigger step: eta must stay below one over lambda max, not lambda min.

18
00:02:11,566 --> 00:02:19,900
The best constant rate balances the two extremes, equal and opposite. Now both modes shrink by 0.88 per step.

19
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:26,600
Even the best constant learning rate is still slow. The ravine itself is the problem.

20
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:33,200
The ratio of the largest to the smallest eigenvalue is the condition number, kappa. Here it is 16.

21
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:40,833
At the best rate the error shrinks by kappa minus one over kappa plus one per step: 15 over 17 here.

22
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:48,833
Iterations grow with kappa: one for a round bowl, over two hundred at kappa 100, over two thousand at 1000.

23
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:57,600
Large condition number means slow convergence even when stable. Momentum and Adam will attack exactly this.

